  "text": "Since the circles with diameters $AP$, $BQ$, and $CR$ intersect at points $X$, $Y$, and $Z$, we can use the fact that the circumcircles of $\\triangle PQR$ and $\\triangle ABQ$, $\\triangle CAQ$, and $\\triangle ABR$ also intersect at $X$, $Y$, and $Z$. This means that $\\triangle XYZ$ is the triangle formed by the midpoints of the sides of $\\triangle ABC$, which is the medial triangle of $\\triangle ABC$.\n\nIn a triangle, the medial triangle (formed by the midpoints of the sides) is similar to the original triangle with a ratio of similarity equal to $\\frac{1}{2}$. The area of the medial triangle is therefore $\\frac{1}{4}$ of the area of the original triangle.\n\nTo find the area of the original triangle, we can use Heron's formula. First, we find the semi-perimeter $s$ of $\\triangle ABC$: \n\n$s = \\frac{13 + 14 + 15}{2} = 21$\n\nSo, Heron's formula says that the area $A$ of $\\triangle ABC$ is \n\n$A = \\sqrt{s(s-13)(s-14)(s-15)} = \\sqrt{21(21-13)(21-14)(21-15)}$\n\n$= \\sqrt{21 \\times 8 \\times 7 \\times 6} = \\sqrt{7056} = 84$\n\nSince the medial triangle has an area of $\\frac{1}{4}$ of the original triangle, the area of $\\triangle XYZ$ is \n\n$\\frac{1}{4} \\times 84 = \\boxed{21}$",
